9.text:
The EJR constraints are driven by the two large cohesive groups: the 6-voter bloc on {x1,x2,x3} (forcing at least one member to receive ≥3 representatives) and the 4-voter bloc on {y4,y5,y6} (forcing at least one member to receive ≥2 representatives). The first constraint is satisfiable with only two x-candidates in W (by routing the third representation through z1 for voters 8-10, or through private y/z candidates for voters 1-3), yielding feasible committees such as {x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} that give voter 1 exactly two approvals. The second constraint forces two seats into the y-block, which cannot be used for voter 1's private approvals and therefore caps the intersection at three (realized by committees such as {x1,x2,x3,y4,y5}).

No smaller value satisfies the 6-voter demand; no larger value satisfies the 4-voter demand. All other cohesive subgroups impose weaker constraints already met by these extremal committees. Hence the exact interval is [2,3].

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H. min 2, max 3}

13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. min 2, max 4

The 6-voter x-coalition (voters 1-3,8-10) is 3-cohesive on {x1,x2,x3}, so EJR requires that some member of the coalition approve at least three winners. This lower bound is met as soon as W contains any two of the x-candidates together with y1: voter 1 then reaches exactly three approvals while the remaining three seats can be filled by two members of {y4,y5,y6} plus one more candidate from the y-bloc (e.g., z1 or z2). The resulting committee {x1,x2,y1,y4,y5} therefore satisfies both the ℓ=3 and ℓ=2 EJR conditions and gives voter 1 four approved members.

The same counting shows that the lower bound cannot fall below two: replacing both x-candidates with non-x candidates leaves every member of the x-coalition with at most two approvals, violating the ℓ=3 requirement. Hence the attainable interval is exactly 2–4.}

