PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core EJR constraints unchanged: n=10/k=5 ⇒ threshold 2ℓ. Sx (v1-3+8-10, size 6) is 3-cohesive on exactly {x1,x2,x3}, requiring |W ∩ {x1,x2,x3}| ≥ 2 (ℓ=3 witness reachable via z1 for v8-10 or private y/z for v1-3). Sy (v4-7, size 4) is 2-cohesive on {y4,y5,y6}, requiring ≥2 seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2}. Voter 1 approves all x-candidates, so ≥2 x-candidates already yields |W ∩ A1| ≥ 2; the two mandatory Sy seats lie outside A1, capping the intersection at ≤3.

Explicit tight examples: {x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} (value 2) and {x1,x2,x3,y4,y5} (value 3). Both satisfy every cohesive-group condition; 0/1 violate Sx ℓ=3, 4/5 violate Sy ℓ=2.

Round-1 consensus strongly supports [2,3] (H). Remaining A claims rest on flawed premise that z1 can substitute for a second y-seat while still freeing an extra A1 slot; explicit enumeration shows this still forces |W ∩ A1| ≤ 3. No new evidence alters the interval. Prior reflection confirmed.}

