14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. min 2, max 4

Colleague 14's construction is decisive: once z1 is admitted as a legitimate way to discharge the ℓ=2 requirement for voters 4-5, the y-bloc no longer forces two full seats from {y4,y5,y6}. The committee {x1,x2,y1,y4,z1} therefore satisfies both large-coalition EJR conditions (the 6-voter x-group receives three approvals via x1,x2 plus the private y1 for voter 1; the 4-voter y-group receives two approvals via y4 and z1) while intersecting voter 1's ballot in exactly four candidates. The symmetric two-x variant {x1,x2,y1,y4,z3} works equally well.  

The lower bound of two remains intact because the 6-voter x-coalition still requires |W ∩ {x1,x2,x3}| ≥ 2; zero or one x-candidate leaves every member of that coalition with at most two approvals. Hence the attainable interval is exactly 2–4.}

11.text:
The retained reflection and the detailed public responses from the prior round already establish the two binding EJR constraints (the size-6, ℓ=3 x-coalition forcing |W ∩ {x1,x2,x3}| ≥ 2, and the size-4, ℓ=2 y-coalition forcing two seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2} outside A1). These together fix the attainable interval for voter 1 at exactly {2,3}, witnessed by the explicit EJR committees {x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} and {x1,x2,x3,y4,y5}.

The three responses still advocating A rest on committees that fail the y-coalition (e.g., {x1,x2,y1,y4,z1} supplies only one y-bloc seat, so voters 4–7 cannot reach ℓ=2). No other interval survives both constraints simultaneously.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H. min 2, max 3}

7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. min 2, max 4

Colleagues 13, 14 and 7 have already identified the key point the H cluster is missing: the ℓ=2 demand for the four-voter y-bloc is satisfiable without consuming two full seats from {y4,y5,y6}. Because voters 4–5 approve z1, a single y-candidate plus z1 already supplies two approved winners for those voters, discharging the EJR witness for the bloc. The remaining seats can therefore accommodate an additional member of A1 (y1 or z3) while still placing at least two x-candidates to meet the ℓ=3 requirement for the six-voter x-bloc.  

Explicit constructions confirm both extremes:  
- {x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} meets every EJR lower bound and intersects A1 in exactly two candidates;  
- {x1,x2,y1,y4,z1} likewise meets both bounds yet intersects A1 in four candidates.  

The stricter “two dedicated y4/y5/y6 seats required” premise used by the H advocates therefore does not hold, and the attainable interval widens to 2–4.}

15.text:
