public_text.7:
{A. min 2, max 4

The size-6 x-coalition still forces |W ∩ {x1,x2,x3}| ≥ 2, locking the lower bound at two approvals for voter 1. On the y side, however, the 4-voter ℓ=2 bloc is not monolithic: voters 4–5 already approve z1, so the witness for that half can be supplied by any single y-candidate (y4 or y5) paired with z1. Voters 6–7 are then covered symmetrically by z2 if needed. This accounting uses only one dedicated seat from {y4,y5,y6} plus the two z-candidates, freeing a fifth seat that can intersect voter 1’s ballot (y1 or z3) while still satisfying both large-coalition EJR witnesses.  

Explicit committees realizing the extremes are therefore {x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} (intersection exactly 2) and {x1,x2,y1,y4,z1} (intersection exactly 4). The stricter “two full y-seats strictly outside A1” premise used by the H cluster does not hold once the partial coverage of z1 is taken into account, so the attainable interval is [2,4].}

public_text.15:
{H. min 2, max 3}

The two binding EJR constraints (size-6 ℓ=3 x-coalition on {x1,x2,x3} and size-4 ℓ=2 y-coalition on {y4,y5,y6}) remain decisive and unchanged. They force |W ∩ {x1,x2,x3}| ≥ 2 (giving voter 1 a lower bound of 2) while simultaneously requiring two seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2} outside A1 (capping the intersection at 3). Both extremes are witnessed by the explicit EJR committees already enumerated ({x1,x2,y4,y5,z1} for 2; {x1,x2,x3,y4,y5} for 3).

The remaining A claims rest on the refuted premise that z1 can discharge the entire Sy ℓ=2 requirement. Explicit check shows z1 covers only voters 4–5; voters 6–7 still need a second seat from {y4,y5,y6,z2}, restoring the two-seat requirement outside A1. Constructions such as {x1,x2,y1,y4,z1} therefore leave voters 6–7 with only one approved winner, violating EJR for Sy. All other cardinalities are likewise ruled out by at least one of the two core constraints. The attainable interval is exactly {2,3}.

public_text.13:
{A. min 2, max 4

Colleague 14’s observation about marginal z2 symmetry is worth pressing further: once z1 is assigned to cover voters 4–5, the remaining ℓ=2 witness for voters 6–7 can be satisfied by a single additional y-seat (y4 or y5) paired with z2, exactly as the split-coalition constructions already show. This accounting does not consume two full seats strictly outside A1, so the extra seat can be allocated inside voter 1’s ballot (y1 or z3) without violating either large-coalition witness.  

The resulting committees {x1,x2,y1,y4,z1} and {x1,x2,y1,y5,z2} both meet the size-6 x-bloc (≥2 x-winners) and give every member of the 4-voter y-bloc at least two approved winners, confirming that 4 is attainable. The lower bound of 2 remains enforced by the x-coalition, and no valid committee reaches 5 or drops to 1 or 0. Hence the attainable interval is [2,4].}

